# Every text file keeps LF line endings in every checkout. Git for Windows checks text out
# with CRLF by default (core.autocrlf=true). Its own bash copes, but any other sh reading that
# working copy (WSL, a Linux container, a Mac on a shared drive) takes each CR as part of a
# command, so `make check` stops at the first script's `set -euo pipefail`. Scripts are not
# the only casualty: the checks read Markdown and JSON as data, and a trailing CR there reads
# as drift (check_prompt_sync.sh) or empties a sed match (test_pre_push_hook.sh). Nothing here
# needs CRLF; binary files are detected and left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
